# What Cloud Build is allowed to see. An ALLOWLIST, deliberately.
#
# WHY NOT JUST LEAN ON .gitignore
#   gcloud falls back to .gitignore semantics when this file is absent, and .env
#   IS gitignored, so it would probably have been excluded. "Probably" is not the
#   standard for a file holding 31 live credentials. This states what goes up
#   rather than inferring what stays behind, and `gcloud meta
#   list-files-for-upload` is run against it before every first deploy.
#
# It also matters for cost and speed: the repo is 332 MB, and the board probe
# needs exactly two files.

# Ignore everything at the root...
/*

# ...then re-admit only the build inputs, directory by directory, because
# gitignore semantics cannot reach into a directory that is itself ignored.
!/cloud
/cloud/*
!/cloud/board-probe
!/cloud/waker-shadow
!/cloud/wa-outbox
!/cloud/board-watcher
!/cloud/agent-waker

!/scripts
/scripts/*
!/scripts/bus.py
!/scripts/board_say.py
!/scripts/state_store.py
!/scripts/board_waker.py
!/scripts/wa_board_outbox.py
!/scripts/wa_notify.py
!/scripts/agent_waker.py
!/scripts/gemini_agent.py
!/scripts/claude_agent.py
!/scripts/fleet_agent.py

# Belt and braces. If a future edit above accidentally re-admits the root, these
# still never go up. An ignore rule that is redundant today is not wasted.
#
# The first version of this file carried `!*.env.example`, meant to protect
# example files from the broad `*.env` rule. It did the opposite of what an
# allowlist is for: it RE-ADMITTED .env.example to the build, which the upload
# listing caught. Nothing here needs an example file, so the negation is gone.
# Printing the upload list is what found it - an allowlist you have not listed
# is a guess.
.env
*.env
.env.*
*.local.md
.git/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
